<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thai National Anthem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">melody completed 6 July <a href="/years/1932">1932</a>; present words adopted 10 December <a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as เพลงชาติไทย, Phleng Chat Thai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Before 1932 the royal anthem &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sansoen-phra-barami">Sansoen Phra Barami</a>&quot; served as Siam's anthem in fact. Phra Chenduriyang, a royal music adviser to the court, recalled in an autobiography written in 1954 that his friend Luang Nithetkolakit of the Royal Siamese Navy had asked him for a new &quot;national anthem of Siam&quot; in the manner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>; loyal to the king, and wary of the rumours of a coup, he put the request off.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thai-national-anthem.jpg" alt="Thai National Anthem" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Music : Phra Chenduriyang and Lyrics : Luang Saranupraphan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phleng_Chat_Thai_Sheet_music_-_Official_version_since_2004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>About five days after the coup of June 1932, Luang Nithetkolakit revealed that he belonged to the People's Party and regretted that the party had no proper song for its success. He asked Phra Chenduriyang again, and this time the request came from the new power in Siam, so the musician reluctantly agreed. He completed the anthem on 6 July 1932 during a tram ride from Tha Tien to Bang Khun Phrom, arranging it for the Royal Siamese Navy Orchestra, and asked that his name not be revealed.</p><p>When the orchestra first played it at the Ananta Samakhom Throne Hall the following week, the newspaper Srikrung named him as composer. His commander, the Minister of the Palace, reprimanded him for making a national anthem on his own authority, and in October 1932 he was laid off from his post and re-entered it at half his former salary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The conspirators of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khana-ratsadon">Khana Ratsadon</a> asked Khun Wichitmatra to write words. His Phleng Chat Sayam had two stanzas of eight verses and had been printed by 29 August 1932. It was popular but never declared official, and it was banned because some lines spoke of seizing power. In 1934 the government held a competition for official lyrics. For a Western-style anthem the committee recommended Phra Chenduriyang's melody, with a revised version of Khun Wichitmatra's words and a second set by the runner-up, Chan Khamwilai. The Cabinet approved these on 20 August 1934.</p><p>The two sets made thirty-two lines in all, and most Thais preferred to sing only Khun Wichitmatra's. In 1939, when Siam became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, a competition produced new lyrics by Luang Saranupraphan, cut from sixteen lines to eight and naming the new country. They were adopted on 10 December 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Regulations of 1935 provided a full-length and a shortened performance of the anthem. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaek-phibunsongkhram">Plaek Phibunsongkhram</a> ordered the anthem played every day at 08:00 and 18:00 and told the public to stand in respect. Under his rule people who did not stand in silence could be fined up to 2,000 baht or jailed for up to a year; the law has since been abolished.</p><p>Today the morning and evening playings match the raising and lowering of the national flag at schools, workplaces and public buildings, and radio and television broadcast it twice a day. Pupils sing it at assembly each school day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thai National Anthem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
